#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0187a97f-58b6-5b57-aaf9-ef89c90383f8" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "63ff719f-1f54-5370-854e-480379c93e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5802" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^2$." :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-1$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (0 - 2)(0 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (-2)(1)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2$, and\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1$.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b6886faa1ecd42e9539b9f6867ff0f629f6ae99366d8b9f397f82c5212ebffd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ca7245-a828-5bbf-9a6a-dcd1938333a2" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "1877c35b-7b3a-55dd-a81e-2b98cf3572bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5805" :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3+x^2-8x-12$, graph the curve $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$. To begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\nx^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}- 8\\begin{vmatrix}-12 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}12\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 4 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x))/(x - 3) &= x^{2} + 4x + 4 \\\\\nf(x) &= (x - 3)(x^{2} + 4x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x+2)^2\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (0 - 3)(0 + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= (-3)(2)^{2} \\\\\n&=-12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-12)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3$, and\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2$.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9f011708eb389611f4c12df70ddfd318039b58b5cbfd7fcb3980a906b0ad75.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0c71e95-0a4a-547f-aaf0-774c6fe694a4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "0ef12435-c090-5766-a58a-f913d6e02421" :question/math-academy-id "e-5807" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(x+1)^2$." :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-1$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (3 - 0)(0 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (3)(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3$, and\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1$.\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*. If we were to expand the parentheses, we'd get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (3 - x)(x + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&=-x^{3} + x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/14/14c1e6701c5f52f37788d22a5e09ba0bc92897ea64fa64ce0f3717115d9604af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c85a009-fe0d-5c56-ac1d-a2a00002f94e" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "35d9d449-6508-5461-89bb-d56c262e6bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5809" :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-x^3 + 2x^2 + 7x + 4$, graph the curve $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$. To begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\nx^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{vmatrix}- 8\\begin{vmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\mid - 1 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x))/(x - 4) &= -x^{2} - 2x - 1 \\\\\n(f(x))/(x - 4) &= - (x^{2} + 2x + 1) \\\\\nf(x) &= - (x - 4)(x^{2} + 2x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = - (x-4)(x+1)^2\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=-1$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= - (0 - 4)(0 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= - (-4)(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,4)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4$, and\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1$.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29091d81f99dc2b99787194bb158cc9a2f850241c5b50ae9ad5ec3f94f11603f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0187a97f-58b6-5b57-aaf9-ef89c90383f8" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8266b81-894e-5d79-94d5-7704a51f679c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df39777d556b266991428a735eeacf46304ce51b2526f402d93e208288cb6a66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113137} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cabc78-b08b-5809-bdb4-c69345cb9c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113138} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c36563-89e6-586f-ae83-004010e49495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1ae0c05746c113c2978afb5c4163dd8e460e36dabd0fbed15bb86b9d9f3aa34b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113139} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa89160-d114-5d7c-91b8-d05050d88ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0d67532b836411bab32b7c2039c4cce75aa22ba1f54ddbc6661b811f023c95f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113140} {:answer/id #uuid "3292067f-f00b-5c28-aec4-62067be83ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/7907855732f87ccac6febf7b90c64b91b4ba5c5ca5b26e3496162839c2d706ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113141}] :answer-field/id #uuid "93c202c0-13aa-577a-bcd3-36c65a6500c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113136}] :question/id #uuid "d74891d1-4f12-551a-bae1-2f54778f9862" :question/math-academy-id "q-28529" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y = f(x)$ where $f(x) = \\frac{1}{8}(x + 4)(x + 2)^{2}$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)"}]}
